1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:25,700
{\an8}Opening example from the earlier structure-check set (Gemini-2.5-Flash, DeepSeek-v4-Flash).
The spatial results later in this video use GPT-6-Luna and DeepSeek-v4.1-Flash.

2
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,024
This iron centre has seven neighbours: it is a pentagonal bipyramid.

3
00:00:04,244 --> 00:00:05,851
Here we replay our recorded run.

4
00:00:06,071 --> 00:00:10,285
Gemini answers the same question from CIF text, from three rendered images,

5
00:00:10,285 --> 00:00:13,487
through an interactive viewer, and with MolCrysKit tools.

6
00:00:13,707 --> 00:00:17,409
From the text and the tools it gets seven, pentagonal bipyramid.

7
00:00:17,629 --> 00:00:23,332
From the images and the viewer it says six, octahedral, and so does DeepSeek in the viewer.

8
00:00:23,552 --> 00:00:25,729
That gap is the question behind CrystalSight.

9
00:00:26,379 --> 00:00:29,743
Before a database structure is used for simulation, someone checks it.

10
00:00:29,963 --> 00:00:34,305
Are hydrogens missing? Is it disordered? What is the metal's coordination?

11
00:00:34,305 --> 00:00:36,300
Can it go straight into DFT or MD?

12
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:41,792
LLM agents are increasingly asked to do this
check, and we can give them the structure as text,

13
00:00:41,792 --> 00:00:45,954
as images rendered with MatterVis, like these, or as an interactive viewer.

14
00:00:46,174 --> 00:00:47,585
We don't know whether those are equivalent.

15
00:00:48,235 --> 00:00:52,208
CrystalSight asks the same questions about the same structures:

16
00:00:52,208 --> 00:00:58,263
67 molecular crystals from the Crystallography Open
Database and MolCrysKit examples, and 247 questions in five families:

17
00:00:58,263 --> 00:01:04,003
hydrogens, disorder, metal coordination, usability for simulation, and pairwise comparison.

18
00:01:04,223 --> 00:01:05,688
Each task has a question,

19
00:01:05,688 --> 00:01:10,728
a JSON answer schema and a gold answer
computed with MolCrysKit plus documented rules.

20
00:01:11,375 --> 00:01:16,816
The model sees one channel at a time: CIF text
with give-away disorder annotations stripped;

21
00:01:16,816 --> 00:01:20,660
the unstripped CIF as a leakage control; three MatterVis renders;

22
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:25,746
or the interactive MatterVis viewer shown here, which it can rotate, zoom and relabel.

23
00:01:25,966 --> 00:01:30,190
Tool access to MolCrysKit is reported separately as a reference upper bound,

24
00:01:30,190 --> 00:01:32,691
because those tools compute the gold answers.

25
00:01:33,110 --> 00:01:36,666
These structure checks put
text ahead of pictures, but

26
00:01:36,666 --> 00:01:39,893
most could be answered
from one line of the file.

27
00:01:39,893 --> 00:01:43,449
So we added two spatial sets
of twenty questions each.

28
00:01:43,449 --> 00:01:46,873
Gold answers are computed
from geometry and stay the

29
00:01:46,873 --> 00:01:49,836
same when every cutoff
moves by five percent.

30
00:01:50,436 --> 00:01:55,888
On the easy set, CIF text
alone scores 0.70 to 0.82.

31
00:01:55,888 --> 00:02:02,387
On the hard set, which asks for
symmetry copies, hydrogen-bond

32
00:02:02,387 --> 00:02:08,468
paths, rings and shell counts,
text drops to 0.20 to 0.43.

33
00:02:09,068 --> 00:02:14,528
Rendered images and the ASCII viewer
stay near chance, at 0.10 to 0.22,

34
00:02:14,528 --> 00:02:19,835
even with a hydrogen-bond overlay
and a distance query in the viewer.

35
00:02:20,435 --> 00:02:24,090
Give the same models six
small geometry tools that

36
00:02:24,090 --> 00:02:27,306
return raw lists, and
both reach about 0.75.

37
00:02:27,306 --> 00:02:31,838
Here one call to the ring tool
is enough for the right answer.

38
00:02:32,438 --> 00:02:37,382
Per family, only the nearest-partner
question is solved without tools.

39
00:02:37,382 --> 00:02:40,630
Disorder replicas stay
hard for every channel.

40
00:02:41,230 --> 00:02:45,715
We found that published hydrogen-bond
tables leaked answers, removed

41
00:02:45,715 --> 00:02:50,068
them, fixed a stripping bug and
re-ran the affected text channels.

42
00:02:50,068 --> 00:02:52,970
The sets are small,
with two models, and the

43
00:02:52,970 --> 00:02:55,872
tools were written for
these question types.

44
00:02:56,697 --> 00:02:59,907
The code, tasks, ground truth and figures are open,

45
00:02:59,907 --> 00:03:02,803
and a mock run like this one needs no API key.

46
00:03:03,023 --> 00:03:04,831
Try your own model, or add a channel.

47
00:03:05,051 --> 00:03:05,892
The link is below.
